# Fluids at Rest: Pressure, Buoyancy, and Bernoulli

> Liquids and gases are fluids: they flow, and even at rest they push
> on everything around them. This lesson builds fluid pressure from the
> ground up — Pascal's law and the hydraulic press, density and relative
> density, Archimedes and the laws of floatation, and Bernoulli's
> principle with its everyday applications and honest caveats.

## 1. Pressure in Fluids

[definition]
**Pressure** is the force acting normally (perpendicularly) on unit
area: $P = F/A$. Its SI unit is the **pascal (Pa)** — 1 Pa = 1 N/m².
Because a fluid yields by flowing, a **fluid at rest** pushes equally
in all directions at a given point.
[/definition]

Three facts summarise the pressure of a fluid at rest:

| Fact | Consequence |
| --- | --- |
| Pressure at a point acts equally in all directions | a deep diver feels the squeeze from every side |
| Pressure increases with depth | dam walls are built thicker at the bottom |
| Pressure depends on depth and density, not container shape | all tubes of a communicating-vessel set stand level |

The pressure at depth h in a liquid of density ρ is

@math label="eq:fluid-pressure"
P = P_{0} + \rho g h
@/math

where $P_0$ is the pressure at the free surface (usually atmospheric)
and $g \approx 9.8\ \mathrm{m/s^{2}}$.

[example]
At 10 m below the surface of water (ρ = 1000 kg/m³) the extra pressure
is $\rho g h = 1000 \times 9.8 \times 10 = 98000$ Pa — roughly one
extra atmosphere for every 10 m of depth. That is why divers' ears
hurt even a few metres down.
[/example]

## 2. Density and Relative Density

[definition]
**Density** is mass per unit volume, $\rho = m/V$, in kg/m³ (or g/cm³,
with 1 g/cm³ = 1000 kg/m³). **Relative density** (specific gravity)
compares a substance with water:

Relative density = density of substance ÷ density of water

It is a pure number with **no unit**, since the two densities cancel.
[/definition]

@chart bar title="Densities of common substances (kg/m³)"
labels:
Cork
Ice
Water
Steel
Mercury

data:
240
920
1000
7800
13600
@/chart

A body with relative density greater than 1 tends to sink in water;
one below 1 tends to float. A **hydrometer** measures relative density
by how deep it sinks — used to test milk, battery acid, and spirit
strength.

## 3. Pascal's Law and Hydraulic Machines

[definition]
**Pascal's law**: pressure applied to an enclosed fluid is transmitted
undiminished to every part of the fluid and to the walls of the
container, always acting at right angles to the surfaces.
[/definition]

Since $P = F/A$ is the same everywhere, a small force on a small
piston can lift a large load on a large piston — the **hydraulic
press**. Take a 100 N push on a piston of area 0.01 m²: it creates a
pressure of 10,000 Pa, which lifts a 5,000 N car resting on a 0.5 m²
piston. The force multiplication is the piston-area ratio:

@math label="eq:hydraulic"
F_{2} = F_{1} \times \frac{A_{2}}{A_{1}}
@/math

| Device | What it does | Where you meet it |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Hydraulic press | multiplies force by A₂/A₁ | baling scrap metal, forging |
| Hydraulic brakes | passes pedal pressure evenly | every car, pedal to four wheels |
| Hydraulic lift or jack | raises heavy loads | garages, service stations |
| Hydraulic excavator | transmits pressure to rams | construction machinery |

[tip]
The gain in force is not free energy: the small piston must travel a
much larger distance, so work in equals work out (friction apart).
Machines multiply force *or* distance — never both.
[/tip]

@diagram tree id="pascal-applications" layout="tree" aria="Applications of Pascal's law"
  "Pascal's law" -> "Hydraulic press"
  "Pascal's law" -> "Hydraulic brakes"
  "Pascal's law" -> "Hydraulic lift and jack"
  "Pascal's law" -> "Hydraulic excavator"
@/diagram

## 4. Atmospheric Pressure

The air above us weighs, pressing with about 101,325 Pa at sea level —
**one atmosphere**, enough to support a 76 cm column of mercury in a
**barometer**. A falling mercury height signals falling pressure and
likely rain.

| Everyday proof | How it works |
| --- | --- |
| Drinking straw | sucking lowers pressure inside the straw; air pressure pushes the drink up |
| Rubber sucker | squeezing out air lets atmospheric pressure pin it to the wall |
| Syringe | pulling the piston lowers the pressure; the liquid is pushed in |
| Why we are not crushed | pressure inside our bodies balances the outside pressure |

[warning]
A vacuum does not *suck*. Sipping a straw creates low pressure inside
it; it is the atmospheric pressure outside that does the pushing.
[/warning]

## 5. Archimedes' Principle

[definition]
**Archimedes' principle**: when a body is wholly or partially immersed
in a fluid, the fluid pushes it upward with a force — the **buoyant
force** or **upthrust** — equal to the weight of the fluid the body
displaces.
[/definition]

[example]
A stone that weighs 8 N in air weighs only 6.5 N when hung in water:
the 1.5 N loss of weight is the upthrust. The stone therefore displaces
water weighing 1.5 N — about 153 g, a volume near 153 cm³.
[/example]

The upthrust grows with the **volume submerged**, the **density of the
fluid** (floating is easier in sea water than fresh), and **g**. Once a
body is fully immersed, going deeper adds *no* further upthrust.

## 6. Floatation: Why Some Things Sink

Whether an object floats is a contest between its weight downward and
the upthrust upward:

| Condition | Result |
| --- | --- |
| Weight greater than the maximum upthrust | body sinks (steel nail in water) |
| Weight equals upthrust, partly submerged | body floats in equilibrium (ship) |
| Weight equals upthrust, wholly submerged | body floats at any depth (fish, submarine) |
| Body density less than fluid density | floats; submerged fraction = relative density |
| Body density greater than fluid density | sinks |

The **law of floatation**: a floating body displaces fluid whose
*weight equals its own weight*. An iceberg's relative density is about
0.92, so roughly nine-tenths of it lies below the surface — only the
tip shows. Ships are built hollow so their *average* density is less
than water, and **Plimsoll lines** on the hull mark the deepest safe
loading in waters of different density.

[important]
An iron ship floats while an iron nail sinks: the ship's shape
displaces a huge volume of water, so the upthrust can match its great
weight. Floating depends on average density, not on the material alone.
[/important]

## 7. Bernoulli's Principle

[definition]
**Bernoulli's principle**: where a fluid flows faster its pressure is
lower, and where it flows slower its pressure is higher. It holds for
streamlined, non-viscous, incompressible flow of a fluid whose density
stays constant from point to point.
[/definition]

@math label="eq:bernoulli"
P + \frac{1}{2}\rho v^{2} + \rho g h = \mathrm{constant}
@/math

Each term is an energy per unit volume: pressure energy, kinetic
energy, and gravitational potential energy. A **venturimeter** turns
the pressure drop in a narrowed pipe into a measure of flow speed.

[warning]
Bernoulli's principle is routinely oversold. "Faster air always has
lower pressure" is not a law of nature — it holds under the streamlined
conditions above. Aeroplane lift is better explained by Newton's third
law: the wing deflects air downward, and the air pushes the wing up.
[/warning]

## 8. Bernoulli at Work: Aerofoils, Atomisers, and Surprises

| Application | How Bernoulli enters |
| --- | --- |
| Aerofoil (wing) | the curved upper surface speeds the airflow, lowering pressure above; together with the wing's angle of attack this lifts the plane |
| Atomiser or perfume sprayer | the bulb speeds air across the tube; the low pressure draws liquid up and scatters it |
| Chimney draft | wind across the top lowers pressure, drawing smoke up |
| Spinning ball (Magnus effect) | spin quickens the airflow on one side; the ball swerves — the swing of a cricket ball |
| Two speeding vehicles | fast air between them is at low pressure; they are drawn together — keep lane distance |
| Roof in a storm | high wind above the roof lowers its pressure; air inside lifts the roof off |

[note]
Blow over the top of a strip of paper and it *rises*: the fast jet
lowers the pressure above while ordinary atmospheric pressure below
stays put — the cheapest classroom demonstration of Bernoulli.
[/note]

[info]
Blood flowing through a narrowed artery speeds up and its pressure
drops — the same physics inside us. Doppler ultrasound exploits these
pressure and speed changes to detect blockages.
[/info]

## 9. Quick Revision

| Item | Remember |
| --- | --- |
| Pressure | P = F/A; unit pascal (N/m²) |
| Pressure at depth | P = P₀ + ρgh — grows with depth and density |
| Pascal's law | enclosed fluid transmits pressure undiminished → press, brakes, lifts |
| Relative density | density ÷ density of water; unit-less; hydrometer measures it |
| Archimedes | upthrust = weight of displaced fluid |
| Floatation | floats when weight = upthrust, i.e. average density below the fluid's |
| Bernoulli | faster flow → lower pressure, for streamlined non-viscous flow |
| Applications | aerofoil lift, atomiser, chimney draft, Magnus effect |
| Caveat | Bernoulli is conditional; lift is also Newton's third law at work |
